title: Subsection 1.1. Live-attenuated ASFV vaccines

The development of live-attenuated ASFV vaccines has been a significant area of research in the field of African swine fever (ASF) control. These vaccines have been generated through the rational deletion of virulence genes from virulent ASFV strains, which has shown promising safety and efficacy in experimental and field conditions [1]. The attenuation process typically involves the removal of genes implicated in viral genome replication and transcription, genes from the multigene family, genes participating in mediating hemadsorption, and other putative cellular attachment factors [2]. While some vaccine candidates are generated by deleting a single viral virulence gene, others involve the simultaneous deletion of multiple genes [3]. In contrast to naturally attenuated vaccines, which have been used but often manifest severe toxic side effects such as fever, abortion, and chronic or persistent infection [4], live-attenuated ASFV vaccines offer a promising alternative for controlling ASF outbreaks. The adaptation of ASFV isolates to cell cultures, such as Vero cells, has also led to the development of attenuated strains with modified viral genomes [5]. Furthermore, live attenuated African swine fever viruses have been used as tools to dissect the mechanisms involved in viral pathogenesis and immune protection [6].



The efficacy and safety profile of live-attenuated ASFV vaccines in field trials has been a subject of interest, with some candidates demonstrating complete protection against lethal challenges with virulent strains [7]. However, concerns remain regarding the severe toxic side effects observed in swine vaccinated with naturally attenuated ASFV vaccines, including fever, abortion, and chronic or persistent infection [2]. Furthermore, the limitations of live-attenuated vaccines are well-documented, particularly in disease-free areas where their application is constrained by the potential for reversion to virulence and the risk of chronic infection [8]. In contrast, gene-deleted LAV vaccine candidates have shown improved safety profiles and efficacy in field trials [2], highlighting a promising approach towards mitigating these risks. Nevertheless, the development and deployment of live-attenuated vaccines remain complex endeavors, requiring careful consideration to ensure their safety and efficacy in diverse field conditions, despite advancements made in this area [9]. 



The use of live-attenuated ASFV vaccines poses several challenges, including the risk of disease transmission to unvaccinated populations in disease-free areas [10]. Furthermore, swine vaccinated with these naturally attenuated vaccines can manifest severe toxic side effects, such as fever, abortion, and chronic or persistent infection. The severity of clinical signs and mortality caused by live-attenuated ASFV vaccines also varies significantly depending on the virulence of the viral strain and the age of the infected pig [11]. A further concern is that attenuated viruses could potentially revert to virulence, posing a significant threat to animal health and complicating disease control efforts [12].


title: Subsection 1.2. Inactivated and protein ASFV vaccine

Inactivated and protein-based African Swine Fever Virus (ASFV) vaccines are being explored as alternatives to live-attenuated vaccines, which have limitations such as severe toxic side effects and the risk of reversion to virulence [7]. These vaccines often involve the use of specific viral proteins identified as critical for inducing a protective immune response, including proteins like M448R and MGF505-7R that are recognized by ASFV-specific T-cells and hold protective potential [2]. Furthermore, self-amplifying mRNA vaccines represent a novel approach, where ASFV and Classical Swine Fever Virus (CSFV) proteins are used to develop subunit vaccines; remarkably, this technology requires only 1-2 μg of self-amplifying mRNA combined with nanoparticle technology to produce immunity in swine [13]. The self-amplifying mRNA-nanoparticle vaccine platform is designed to express multiple viral proteins, aiming to induce both cellular and antibody responses, and has shown promise in preliminary studies by overcoming the limitations of traditional inactivated vaccines that have not been widely successful due to the complexity of ASFV and the need for a robust immune response [15].



In comparison to other vaccine types, inactivated and protein ASFV vaccines have a relatively established efficacy profile, but their safety profiles are less robust compared to some newer technologies. For instance, live-attenuated vaccines (LAVs) for ASF have demonstrated protective effects [16] but come with significant limitations, including severe toxic side effects such as fever, abortion, and chronic or persistent infection in swine vaccinated with naturally attenuated vaccines. In contrast, gene-deleted live-attenuated vaccines have shown promising safety and efficacy [1], providing complete protection against lethal challenges with virulent ASFV strains by deleting specific virulence genes. Furthermore, self-amplifying mRNA vaccines have been shown to induce robust immune responses and protection against viral challenges in pigs [17], requiring only 1-2 μg of mRNA when combined with nanoparticle technology, which could offer advantages over traditional mRNA vaccines, including a simpler manufacturing process and stability at refrigerator temperatures.



The development of inactivated and protein ASFV vaccines has sparked interest in exploring adjuvant-based formulations to enhance their immunogenicity. The integration of self-amplifying mRNA nanoparticles (sa mRNA-NP) has shown promise in boosting immune responses against ASFV, allowing for the use of small mRNA amounts and simplifying manufacture and storage [17]. Furthermore, the incorporation of mannose-modified lipid nanoparticles (LNPs) can facilitate effective antigen presentation and dendritic cell maturation, potentially enhancing both humoral and cellular immune responses [14]. These advancements have significant implications for improving upon traditional live-attenuated vaccines, which are limited by severe toxic side effects such as fever, abortion, and chronic or persistent infection [11]. Gene-deleted live-attenuated vaccines have demonstrated safety and efficacy, but the search for optimal adjuvants continues to improve their immune response and reduce potential side effects [4].


title: Subsection 1.3. DNA and virus-vectored ASFV vaccines

The mechanism of action of DNA and virus-vectored ASFV vaccines involves inducing a protective immune response against the disease. For instance, DNA vaccines work by targeting ASFV antigens to SLA-II bearing cells, thereby enhancing DNA immunization and eliciting an effective immune response [11]. Conversely, virus-vectored vaccines, such as those using vesicular stomatitis virus (VSV) or attenuated ASFV viruses, express ASFV antigens which then stimulate an immune response against the virus. Some studies have shown that these types of vaccines can be highly protective, with a pool of eight virally vectored ASF antigens inducing a robust immune response in pigs and providing protection against fatal ASF disease [16]. However, as seen with attenuated ASFV vaccines, this approach may also come with significant drawbacks, including severe toxic side effects such as fever, abortion, and chronic or persistent infection [1].



The efficacy and safety profiles of virus-vectored ASFV vaccines have been evaluated in various preclinical and field trials, yielding mixed results. While recombinant attenuated ASFV strains (e.g., those developed through genetic modification) [18] show promise but their safety and efficacy remain unclear, a live attenuated vaccine candidate, ASFV-G-ΔI177L, has demonstrated safety and efficacy in protecting European and native pig breeds against circulating field strains of ASFV [19]. Additionally, DNA immunization targeting ASFV antigens to SLA-II bearing cells has shown partial protection against lethal ASFV challenge in the absence of antibodies [20], highlighting the need for further optimization. Furthermore, a vesicular stomatitis virus-based ASFV vaccine has shown acceptable safety in mice, although its efficacy in larger animal models and field conditions is still under investigation. The Lv17/WB/Rie1 vaccine candidate has also been evaluated in wild boar, showing that overdose and repeated doses do not significantly compromise safety [21], although long-term effects and broader field application need further study.



The production and delivery of DNA and virus-vectored ASFV vaccines pose significant challenges. One major concern is the potential for severe toxic side effects, such as fever, abortion, and chronic or persistent infection, which have been observed in swine vaccinated with naturally attenuated viruses [1]. The use of live attenuated vaccines (LAV) also has limitations, particularly in disease-free areas, due to the potential risks associated with the live virus [8], and requires careful handling and storage to maintain their efficacy and safety, which can be challenging in field conditions [22]. Furthermore, gene-deleted live-attenuated vaccines involve complex genetic modifications that can be time-consuming and costly to develop and validate [4], while also necessitating stringent safety protocols due to the risk of reversion to virulence [4].


title: Subsection 1.4. RNA ASFV vaccines (previously mRNA-based)

The development of RNA-based ASFV vaccines is a promising area of research, driven by the need for new technologies to combat this highly infectious and deadly disease. Traditional inactivated and live attenuated vaccines have shown limited success and significant side effects (6), making it crucial to explore alternative vaccine platforms such as subunit vaccines, DNA vaccines, and vector virus vaccines (2). Recent advances in gene-editing technologies like CRISPR/Cas9 have enabled the screening of potential immunoprotective antigens and development of effective ASFV vector vaccines (23), which could also inform RNA-based vaccine strategies. Furthermore, a deeper understanding of key antigens and immune mechanisms is essential for designing effective ASFV vaccines, including RNA-based vaccines that target specific ASFV antigens (23). The encouraging results from DNA vaccines encoding multiple ASFV antigens (24), which have shown some protective efficacy, suggest that RNA-based vaccines could also be effective if they are designed to target the right antigens and induce the appropriate immune responses.



Notably, mRNA-based ASFV vaccines offer several advantages over other vaccine types in terms of efficacy, safety, and production costs. They can be manufactured in a cell-free manner [15], allowing for rapid, scalable, and cost-effective production, which is a significant advantage over conventional vaccine production methods. Unlike some viral vaccines, mRNA vaccines do not integrate into the genome [15], reducing concerns about insertional mutagenesis and enhancing safety profiles. Furthermore, mRNA vaccines have shown promising safety profiles with only mild or moderate adverse events in clinical trials [15], although optimization of mRNA antigens and delivery vehicle components is still necessary. The ability to encode multiple antigens using advanced technologies like mRNA vaccines also offers the potential for strengthening the immune response against resilient pathogens like ASFV [17]. This feature is not always available in traditional vaccine types, which may explain why LAVs and other vaccine platforms being explored for ASF often come with limitations in terms of safety and stability [18].



The potential for ASFV vaccines to be adapted for use in different animal species is a crucial consideration, given that the virus infects all members of the family Suidae (including domestic pigs, wild boars (*Sus scrofa ferus*), warthogs (*Phacochoerus aethiopicus*), and bushpigs (*Potamochoerus porcus*)) [25]. mRNA vaccines for ASFV offer a promising approach in this regard, as the immunogenicity of specific viral proteins can be tested and potentially adapted across suid species by targeting common viral antigens [26]. Moreover, the development of mRNA vaccines using self-amplifying mRNA-nanoparticle technology has shown robust immune responses and protection in pigs, suggesting that these vaccines could be adapted for use in other suid species given their broad applicability and flexibility in design [17]. Furthermore, the use of mRNA vaccines may offer a safer alternative to live-attenuated vaccines, which have been associated with severe toxic side effects in some animal species [13], making them more suitable for adaptation across different species.


title: Subsection 1.5. The challenges of developing ASFV vaccines

The complexity of the ASFV genome poses significant challenges to vaccine development, particularly due to its large size, ranging from 170 to 193 kilobase pairs (kbp) and containing 150 to 190 open reading frames (ORFs) [27]. The identification of protective antigens is further complicated by the presence of over 100 viral proteins in ASFV-infected cells, with at least 68 of these proteins found within the viral virions, many of which have no known or attributed functions [4]. Moreover, the classification of ASFV into 24 genotypes based on the sequence of its major capsid protein, p72, highlights the need for a genotype-specific approach to vaccine development. While live-attenuated vaccines (LAV) may offer some benefits, their limitations in disease-free areas due to potential risks and side effects such as fever, abortion, and chronic or persistent infection, must also be considered.



The development of effective ASFV vaccines is hindered by a limited understanding of immune responses to the virus, which poses significant challenges in designing vaccines that can induce robust and protective immunity [28]. The complex clinical outcomes associated with ASFV infection, ranging from peracute to chronic, depending on strain virulence and host age [29], further complicate this issue. Moreover, the large genome of the virus (comprising over 100 identified viral proteins) [30] adds complexity to understanding how the immune system responds to ASFV infection. As a result, current vaccine candidates often exhibit severe toxic side effects, such as fever, abortion, and chronic or persistent infection, highlighting the need for a better comprehension of the immune response to develop safer vaccines. The immune crosstalk between adaptive and innate immunity, including the recognition of specific antigens by ASFV-specific T-cells [2], remains an area of ongoing research in the quest for effective vaccine development.



The development of ASFV vaccines is hindered by significant regulatory and logistical hurdles. Live-attenuated vaccines, which have shown promise in protecting against ASFV [11], pose challenges due to their potential for reversion to virulence and the risk of chronic or persistent infection, making them problematic for use in disease-free areas. Furthermore, live-attenuated vaccines are limited by severe toxic side effects such as fever, abortion, and chronic infection in vaccinated swine [31], complicating their deployment and acceptance. Developing and distributing ASFV vaccines also requires navigating complex logistical challenges, including stringent biosafety standards and the need for stable vaccine formulations that can be easily manufactured and distributed, particularly in regions with limited infrastructure [11]. Additionally, the development of novel vaccine technologies, such as mRNA vaccines [15], must contend with regulatory frameworks that are often not fully adapted to these new approaches, adding an extra layer of complexity to the deployment process.


title: Subsection 2.1. The principal pharmacology of mRNA vaccine

The mechanism of action of mRNA vaccines involves introducing a short-lived synthetically created fragment of the RNA sequence of a pathogen into the individual being vaccinated, where it is taken up by dendritic cells and translated into viral antigens [32]. This process begins when the co-formulated mRNA, encapsulated in lipid nanoparticles to protect the RNA strands and facilitate their absorption into the cells [33], instructs the host cells to build proteins that match up with parts of the pathogen called antigens. The engineered mRNA uses intracellular translation machinery to synthesize antigenic protein, which undergoes post-translational modifications, degradation by the proteasome system, and presentation to the immune system [35]. As a result, the immune system sees these foreign antigens as invaders, dispatching defenders called antibodies and T-cells, and training the immune system for potential future attacks. Notably, the mRNA fragments are degraded within a few days of introduction and do not affect the body's genomic DNA, located separately in the cell nucleus [36].



mRNA-based ASFV vaccines exhibit several key characteristics and properties that contribute to their efficacy and safety profile. The use of mannose-modified lipid nanoparticles (LNPs) in these vaccines enhances antigen presentation, facilitates dendritic cell maturation [17], and leads to strong IgG titers and activation of CD4+ and CD8+ T-cells. Furthermore, self-amplifying mRNA vaccines for ASFV have been shown to be highly effective, requiring only 1-2 μg of mRNA to produce immunity in swine, while also being stable at refrigerator temperatures. These vaccines can express multiple viral proteins, such as those from ASFV and Classical Swine Fever Virus (CSFV), to induce both cellular and antibody responses [14]. Moreover, mRNA vaccines offer several advantages over traditional vaccine technologies, including minimal risk of insertional mutagenesis, high safety profiles, effectiveness, rapid scalability in production, and cost-effectiveness [15].



Unlike traditional vaccine types, mRNA vaccines offer several advantages in terms of production, delivery, and immune response induction. Notably, they provide rapid scalability in production [14], which is critical for responding quickly to outbreaks like ASF, whereas traditional vaccines often require more time-consuming processes. Furthermore, mRNA vaccines have a high safety profile with minimal risk of insertional mutagenesis [15], compared to some traditional vaccine types, and do not exhibit severe toxic side effects such as fever, abortion, and chronic or persistent infection, which can be observed with live-attenuated ASF vaccines [16]. In terms of immune response induction, mRNA vaccines have been shown to elicit both humoral and cellular responses, including the activation of CD4+ and CD8+ T-cells and the elicitation of strong IgG titers [15], and can be designed to be more efficient using self-amplifying mRNA technology combined with nanoparticle technology, requiring only 1-2 μg of mRNA [17]. Additionally, mRNA vaccines can be quickly adapted and produced using existing platforms, unlike live-attenuated ASF vaccines which require complex attenuation processes [20], and can be stabilized at refrigerator temperatures, simplifying storage and distribution compared to some traditional vaccines.


title: Subsection 3.1. mRNA vaccine can induce powerful Thl-bias immune response and memory

The mechanism by which mRNA vaccines induce Th1-dominated immune responses is multifaceted and involves the activation of multiple immune pathways. Specifically, the stimulation of pattern recognition receptors such as toll-like receptors (TLRs) [37] and retinoic acid-inducible gene I (RIG-I) allows for the recognition of multiple vaccine components and leads to the production of proinflammatory cytokines and type I interferons (IFNs). This, in turn, contributes to the characteristic Th1 response, which is marked by the production of cytokines such as IFN-γ and IL-2 [22], indicative of a Th1-skewed immune profile. Furthermore, the lipid nanoparticle (LNP) component of mRNA vaccines plays a crucial role in innate immune activation, facilitating the infiltration and activation of neutrophils, monocytes, and dendritic cells, as well as the detection of type I IFN-related cytokines [14]. Ultimately, this Th1-dominated immune response is critical for effective intracellular pathogen responses, including CD8+ T cell-mediated immunity and B cell class switching.



In comparison to other vaccine types, mRNA vaccines stand out for their ability to induce a powerful Th1-bias immune response and memory (38). Unlike peptide-based vaccination approaches, which are often limited by their requirement for specific HLA molecules and the risk of antigen loss due to immune escape (38), mRNA vaccines can combine mRNAs encoding different antigens, allowing for a broad and polyclonal immune response. Furthermore, mRNA vaccines do not require coadministration with an adjuvant, likely due to their inherent ability to activate strong innate immune responses (15). Additionally, the administration route of mRNA vaccines, such as intramuscular or intradermal delivery, can influence the duration of antigen expression and the resulting immune response, including high antibody titers and germinal center B cell responses (15). Notably, in comparison to homologous or heterologous vaccination strategies using protein-based vaccines, mRNA vaccines can induce balanced IgG1/IgG2a responses and high hemagglutination inhibition titers, indicating a strong humoral immune response.



The mRNA vaccine approach could offer a powerful tool for controlling African Swine Fever (ASF), a highly lethal and rapidly spreading disease. The ability of mRNA vaccines to induce a Th1-biased immune response and memory [10] provides a strong potential benefit for ASF control. By leveraging the advantages of mRNA technology, such as faster production and lower risk of contamination compared to traditional virus-based vaccines [13], it may be possible to rapidly develop and deploy effective vaccine strategies against ASF. Furthermore, the use of mRNA vaccines could potentially offer enhanced protective benefits by targeting specific viral antigens, similar to the promising results seen with live attenuated viruses (LAVs) [8]. Overall, the integration of mRNA technology into disease control measures for ASF and other food-animal diseases has the potential to revolutionize this field [4].


title: Subsection 3.2. mRNA vaccine enables the development of multivalent ASFV vaccines

The concept of multivalent mRNA vaccines for African Swine Fever (ASF) presents a promising approach to enhancing protection against different strains of ASFV. mRNA vaccines, which have emerged as promising candidates for ASF due to their minimal risk of insertional mutagenesis, high safety profiles, effectiveness, rapid scalability in production, and cost-effectiveness [4], can be designed to express multiple viral proteins. This ability allows for the inclusion of various antigens or viral proteins in a single vaccine formulation, thereby enhancing the immune response against different strains of ASFV [32]. The use of self-amplifying mRNA-nanoparticle technology, similar to that used in COVID-19 vaccines [17], can further facilitate the development of multivalent mRNA vaccines. By incorporating multiple viral proteins, such as those identified as critical for inducing a protective immune response in pigs [39], these vaccines offer broad protection against various ASFV strains. Furthermore, the potential of existing mRNA vaccine candidates, such as mRNA/Man-LNP (mannose-modified lipid nanoparticles), to elicit both humoral and cellular immune responses suggests that multivalent formulations could be developed for enhanced efficacy [40].



Developing multivalent mRNA vaccines for ASFV poses several technical challenges, including identifying critical viral proteins that induce a protective immune response (Choi et al., 2018). For example, proteins previously identified as crucial for eliciting protective antibodies in animals vaccinated with live-attenuated vaccines must be tested for their ability to confer protection when presented in an mRNA-based vaccine format. Additionally, the use of self-amplifying mRNA technology can reduce the amount of mRNA required for vaccine efficacy, as demonstrated by studies using as little as 1-2 μg of self-amplifying mRNA combined with nanoparticle technology (Morton et al., 2013). Furthermore, ensuring the stability and simplicity of manufacturing are essential considerations, particularly given the ability of self-amplifying mRNA-nanoparticle vaccines to remain stable at refrigerator temperatures and benefit from a simplified manufacturing process due to the stabilizing effect of the nanoparticle's outer cationic charge (Morton et al., 2013). Moreover, it is crucial that the vaccine platform can induce both cellular and antibody responses, as demonstrated by its efficacy in inducing these responses and providing protection against viral challenge, a requirement that must be carefully evaluated through testing (Li et al., 2020). Finally, the design of multivalent vaccines must also consider the potential for immune interference between different vaccine components, which necessitates careful selection and formulation of the viral proteins included to maintain overall efficacy (Srivastava et al., 2017).



The development of mRNA vaccines offers a promising solution for controlling African Swine Fever Virus (ASFV), a highly variable and rapidly spreading disease. The flexibility and faster response offered by mRNA vaccines could be crucial in combating ASFV, particularly considering its high variability [14]. Additionally, mRNA vaccines can induce an immune response with minimal viral material, making them cleaner and easier to produce compared to traditional vaccine development methods [13]. Furthermore, the use of mRNA technology adds another tool to the toolbox for combating diseases like ASF, potentially providing a revolutionary approach in vaccine development [42]. Importantly, mRNA vaccines do not alter an animal's genes, as the mRNA acts as an intermediate between the gene and the protein, making it a safe option for animal health [13]. The promising results of mRNA vaccines in other animal diseases suggest their potential applicability to ASFV control [28], which could complement or potentially replace traditional live-attenuated virus (LAV) vaccines that have shown promise in ASFV control [8].


title: Subsection 3.3. Flexible utility and alternation of ASFV antigens

The flexibility in antigen selection, design, and optimization using mRNA vaccines offers a significant advantage in the development of ASFV vaccines (13). This is achieved through the incorporation of transgene sequences that express specific viral proteins, such as those identified as critical for inducing a protective immune response. The use of self-amplifying mRNA technology further enhances the vaccine's efficacy and stability by enabling the expression of multiple viral proteins with minimal mRNA dosage, requiring only 1-2 μg when combined with nanoparticle technology to produce immunity in swine (e.g., [43]). Moreover, mRNA vaccines can be rapidly adapted to include different antigen combinations, such as those from ASFV and Classical Swine Fever Virus (CSFV), making them versatile tools for combating multiple swine diseases. Furthermore, the mRNA vaccine platform can be optimized to elicit both humoral and cellular immune responses, as demonstrated by an ASF p30 mRNA vaccine candidate using mannose-modified lipid nanoparticles (LNPs) that activated CD4+ and CD8+ T-cells and elicited strong IgG titers [40].



The ease of adaptation to emerging or variant strains of ASFV is a significant advantage of mRNA vaccines, given their flexible design and rapid scalability in production [42]. This allows for quick updates to the vaccine sequence to match new viral strains. Furthermore, self-amplifying mRNA technology enables the incorporation of multiple viral proteins from ASFV and CSFV, enabling a broader immune response and potential cross-protection against different strains. In contrast to gene-deleted live-attenuated vaccines, which require significant genetic modifications to the viral genome [41], mRNA vaccines can be easily modified to include new antigen sequences without altering the viral genome itself, making them highly adaptable.



The potential benefits of mRNA vaccines in responding to evolving ASFV threats are multifaceted. One significant advantage is their rapid scalability in production, which is crucial for quickly responding to emerging and evolving ASFV strains [14]. Moreover, these vaccines can be easily modified to incorporate different ASFV antigens, allowing for flexible adaptation to new viral strains, such as the incorporation of novel glycoprotein E (gpE) or p72 antigens [23]. This flexibility in antigen design is complemented by the strong humoral and cellular immune responses elicited by mRNA vaccines, which are essential for providing comprehensive protection against ASFV [39]. Additionally, the use of self-amplifying mRNA technology requires only a small amount of mRNA, making it efficient and potentially cost-effective for large-scale vaccination efforts [14], while also simplifying storage and distribution requirements with stable refrigerated temperatures [15]. Furthermore, the mRNA vaccine platform can be designed to express multiple viral proteins, allowing for a broad immune response and potentially offering cross-protection against different ASFV genotypes [6].


title: Subsection 3.4. Short development cycle and Fast manufacture and reducing cost

The accelerated timeline for developing mRNA-based ASFV vaccines is a significant advantage of this technology. mRNA vaccines can be developed rapidly due to their straightforward design and production process, which involves synthesizing the mRNA sequence encoding the desired antigen, allowing for a significantly faster development time compared to traditional vaccine development methods [41]. Furthermore, the use of mRNA technology enables quick adaptation to new viral strains or mutations, as only the mRNA sequence needs to be updated, which can be done rapidly without the need for extensive retooling of manufacturing processes [42]. Additionally, the elimination of live pathogen handling in mRNA vaccines simplifies the development and manufacturing process, reducing the time and cost associated with biosafety level 3 or 4 facilities [14]. As advanced technologies continue to play a crucial role in designing various potential vaccine candidates for ASF, this highlights the potential for rapid development and deployment, further underscoring the benefits of mRNA-based vaccines in addressing emerging viral threats [44].



In comparison to traditional live-attenuated vaccines, mRNA vaccines offer a significant advantage in terms of production costs and timelines. The development and manufacture of mRNA vaccines can be achieved more quickly than their traditional counterparts, which is particularly crucial for rapid response to outbreaks (García et al., 2020). For example, the production timeline for traditional live-attenuated African Swine Fever vaccines is often delayed by complex processes such as attenuating the virus, whereas mRNA vaccines utilizing self-amplifying mRNA technology with nanoparticle technology can be produced with minimal material requirements of only 1-2 μg (Lammers et al., 2018). Furthermore, traditional live-attenuated vaccines for African Swine Fever have been associated with severe toxic side effects, including fever, abortion, and chronic or persistent infection, which complicates their use and may increase the overall cost of vaccination programs due to extensive safety testing and potential side effect management. In contrast, mRNA vaccines can be stabilized at refrigerator temperatures, simplifying storage and distribution and reducing logistical costs compared to traditional vaccines that may require more stringent storage conditions (Roldan et al., 2019).



The rapid development cycle of mRNA vaccines presents a significant advantage in responding to emerging or epidemic situations, such as the African Swine Fever (ASF) outbreak. As they do not require the cultivation of live viruses or the production of viral proteins [13], mRNA vaccines can be rapidly developed and manufactured, enabling swift adaptation to new viral strains or mutations, which is essential for controlling outbreaks like ASF. Furthermore, mRNA vaccines can be produced at a lower cost compared to traditional vaccine methods [15], facilitating widespread vaccination efforts during epidemics while also eliminating the need for handling live pathogens, thus reducing biosafety risks associated with vaccine production [14]. The ability of mRNA vaccines to induce robust immune responses and their efficacy can be evaluated quickly [15] aids in rapid deployment, making them a valuable tool in responding to emerging or epidemic situations. 


title: Subsection 4.1. Antigen selection and design: determining the specificity of ASFV mRNA vaccines 

The selection of antigens for ASFV mRNA vaccines is a critical step in determining their specificity, influenced by the need to induce both humoral and cellular immune responses. Effective antigen presentation and the maturation of dendritic cells (DCs) are crucial, as exemplified by the development of an ASF p30 mRNA vaccine candidate using mannose-modified lipid nanoparticles (LNPs), which demonstrated improved immunogenicity [14]. The choice of antigens must also consider viral proteins specifically recognized during the protective antibody response elicited in animals efficaciously vaccinated with live-attenuated vaccines (LAVs), such as those identified by scientists from the ARS as critical for inducing a protective immune response in pigs, which are being tested as experimental subunit vaccines using self-amplifying mRNA technology [8]. Furthermore, the design of mRNA vaccines involves targeting specific viral genes and proteins implicated in viral pathogenesis and immune protection, with insights gained from gene-deleted live-attenuated vaccines that have been developed by deleting virulence genes from virulent ASFV strains [2]. The immunogenicity of ASFV proteins, such as those from the multigene family and other virulence genes, also plays a significant role in antigen selection, with these proteins often targeted for deletion in live-attenuated vaccines and considered for mRNA vaccine design [4]. Additionally, the use of self-amplifying mRNA technology allows for the expression of multiple viral proteins, enhancing the immune response, as explored in experimental subunit vaccines using self-amplifying mRNA-nanoparticle technology that can express up to 20 viral proteins [15].



The development of ASF mRNA vaccines requires careful consideration of antigen specificity, purity, and safety to ensure their efficacy and prevent unwanted immune responses. This involves selecting specific viral proteins that are critical for inducing a protective immune response, such as the immunogenic protein p30 [23], which has been identified as a promising candidate. Furthermore, targeting specific viral genes, like the I177L gene whose deletion has been shown to result in sterile immunity against the current epidemic Eurasia strain [18], is crucial for ensuring antigen specificity. The use of self-amplifying mRNA technology can enhance antigen presentation and facilitate dendritic cell maturation, leading to strong humoral and cellular immune responses while maintaining safety profiles [14]. Additionally, the design of mRNA vaccines minimizes the risk of insertional mutagenesis, ensures high safety profiles, effectiveness, and rapid scalability in production [15], and their stability at refrigerator temperatures, when combined with nanoparticle technology, is a critical factor for ensuring both safety and efficacy [15].



The optimization of antigens for inclusion in mRNA vaccines is a critical aspect of inducing an effective immune response against ASFV. The selection of specific viral proteins, such as those involved in virulence gene expression (e.g., the deletion of the I177L gene [18]) or commonly recognized by T-cells (e.g., M448R and MGF505-7R [24]), is essential for facilitating strong antibody responses and activating CD4+ and CD8+ T-cells, as observed with the p30 protein in mRNA vaccines [15]. Furthermore, the incorporation of multiple viral proteins through self-amplifying mRNA technology can enhance the immune response by expressing 20 viral proteins [28], making it an attractive approach for testing efficacy in inducing both cellular and antibody responses.


title: Subsection 4.2. RNA sequence design: increasing the expression of antigen

Designing optimal RNA sequences for ASFV mRNA vaccines is crucial to elicit strong immune responses against this highly contagious virus. To achieve this, researchers can employ self-amplifying mRNA technology derived from non-structural genes of alphaviruses [14], which reduces the amount of mRNA required for immunization and makes the vaccine more efficient and stable at refrigerator temperatures. This technology, combined with mannose-modified lipid nanoparticles (LNPs), can further enhance antigen presentation and facilitate dendritic cell maturation, thereby increasing the effectiveness of the vaccine [14]. Moreover, optimal RNA sequence design involves identifying and incorporating critical viral proteins recognized during the protective immune response, such as p30 antigens from ASFV, which have been identified as promising targets for mRNA vaccine development [6]. Studies have demonstrated that the mRNA/Man-LNP vaccine candidate can elicit both humoral and cellular immune responses, including strong IgG titers and activation of CD4+ and CD8+ T-cells, indicating effective antigen expression and presentation [17].



When designing mRNA sequences to increase the expression of an antigen, considerations must be taken into account to optimize translation efficiency, stability, and delivery to target cells. For instance, using self-amplifying mRNA-nanoparticle technology can simplify manufacture and maintain stability at refrigerator temperatures [17], while also requiring only 1-2 μg of mRNA when combined with nanoparticle technology, making the vaccine more efficient [17]. Furthermore, the use of mannose-modified lipid nanoparticles (LNPs) can facilitate effective antigen presentation and dendritic cell maturation, which is critical for eliciting strong immune responses, including both humoral and cellular immunity [37]. Additionally, optimizing the delivery system, such as using lipid nanoparticles, can enhance the targeting of mRNA to specific cells, such as dendritic cells, thereby improving the immune response [14]. This approach also allows for the design of mRNA vaccines that express multiple viral proteins, which can be delivered efficiently using nanoparticle technology, and has been shown to induce a protective immune response against specific viruses, such as ASFV [41].



The design of RNA sequences has a profound impact on vaccine efficacy, particularly in mRNA vaccines. By carefully crafting the sequence to incorporate specific viral proteins, researchers have been able to elicit strong immune responses. For example, the incorporation of viral proteins such as p30 (ASFV) [2] into mRNA sequences has shown effective antigen presentation, dendritic cell maturation, and the activation of CD4+ and CD8+ T-cells. Furthermore, optimizing mRNA sequences to include critical viral proteins identified by previous studies can lead to enhanced protective immunity, as seen in the inclusion of proteins like M448R and MGF505-7R [2], which are commonly recognized by ASFV-specific T-cells. Additionally, the use of self-amplifying mRNA technology stabilized by attachment to nanoparticles [35] and the optimization of delivery systems, such as mannose-modified lipid nanoparticles [14], also play a crucial role in enhancing antigen expression and vaccine efficacy.


title: Subsection 4.3. Delivery systems and adjuvants: strengthening the immune response and memory

The development of mRNA vaccines for African Swine Fever (ASF) has led to the exploration of various delivery systems, including mannose-modified lipid nanoparticles (LNPs). These LNPs have been shown to enhance antigen presentation and facilitate dendritic cell maturation, resulting in strong IgG titers and activation of CD4+ and CD8+ T-cells [16]. Furthermore, self-amplifying mRNA vaccines are being investigated, utilizing nanoparticle technology to stabilize the mRNA and enable lower doses, as well as stability at refrigerator temperatures. This approach involves attaching the self-amplifying mRNA to the surface of nanoparticles, simplifying manufacturing processes [17]. The use of lipid nanoparticles in these mRNA vaccines is critical for effective delivery and presentation of the antigen, which is essential for eliciting both humoral and cellular immune responses against ASFV [14].



The role of adjuvants in enhancing immune responses to ASFV mRNA vaccines has been a critical area of investigation. The use of mannose-modified lipid nanoparticles (LNPs) as an adjuvant has been shown to enhance antigen presentation and facilitate dendritic cell maturation, leading to strong IgG titers and activation of CD4+ and CD8+ T-cells. Moreover, the incorporation of specific viral proteins identified as critical for inducing a protective immune response into mRNA vaccines, when combined with nanoparticle technology, can significantly enhance the immunogenicity of the vaccine [41]. Notably, self-amplifying mRNA vaccines stabilized by attachment to nanoparticles require only 1-2 μg of mRNA to produce immunity in swine, indicating the potential of nanoparticle technology as an effective adjuvant [15].



The incorporation of novel delivery systems and adjuvants, such as self-amplifying mRNA-nanoparticles, into ASFV vaccine development has the potential to significantly enhance immune responses and memory. This is particularly evident in the context of gene-deleted live-attenuated ASFV vaccines, which have shown promising safety and efficacy profiles by deleting virulence genes, thereby reducing adverse effects and enhancing protection (2). Moreover, the use of mRNA vaccine technology, either alone or complemented with traditional approaches, may offer improved safety profiles compared to live-attenuated vaccines, known to cause severe toxic side effects in swine (15). By enabling the design of vaccines that encode specific antigens recognized by ASFV-specific T-cells, mRNA vaccines have the potential to further enhance the adaptive immune response and lead to better protection against the virus (32). Furthermore, the successful demonstration of complete protection against lethal challenge with a single dose of a gene-deleted ASFV vaccine (2) suggests that similar approaches using mRNA vaccines could yield high efficacy.


title: Subsection 5.1. Future Prospects and Conclusions

The development of ASFV mRNA vaccines represents a promising direction for future research, offering potential solutions to the ongoing challenges of African Swine Fever outbreaks (11). To fully realize their benefits, future directions include optimizing vaccine formulations and delivery systems to enhance efficacy and safety in various pig breeds and under different environmental conditions. Furthermore, researchers are actively exploring the use of mRNA vaccines to express ASFV antigens, which could provide a more targeted and efficient immune response compared to traditional vaccine approaches (32). Additionally, investigating different adjuvants and delivery methods, such as nasal delivery, may also be crucial for enhancing mucosal immunity and protecting against the virus (24). By pursuing these avenues of research, it is likely that ASFV mRNA vaccines will play an increasingly important role in mitigating the impact of African Swine Fever outbreaks.



The development of mRNA-based vaccines for African Swine Fever (ASF) control faces several ongoing research challenges. One significant hurdle is identifying and validating the critical viral proteins that induce a protective immune response, which involves extensive research to determine which proteins are most effective in eliciting immunity [8]. Furthermore, the use of self-amplifying mRNA technology requires less mRNA and is stable at refrigerator temperatures, but its efficacy and safety need further validation, particularly when used in novel subunit vaccine formulations. Ensuring the stability and delivery of the mRNA is another challenge, with the development of self-amplifying mRNA-nanoparticle technology being explored as a potential solution [17]. Additionally, concerns regarding post-vaccination complications and post-viral shedding necessitate careful consideration and further testing to ensure the safety of mRNA-based vaccines. Ongoing research aims to address these challenges through studies on the immunogenicity of ASFV proteins and the evaluation of self-amplifying mRNA-nanoparticle technology for its ability to induce cellular and antibody responses [39]. Despite these challenges, advances in this area are expected to provide valuable insights into the development and deployment of mRNA-based vaccines for ASF control.



In conclusion, the development of mRNA vaccines against African Swine Fever (ASF) holds great promise due to their minimal risk of insertional mutagenesis [15], high safety profiles, effectiveness, rapid scalability in production, and cost-effectiveness. The use of self-amplifying mRNA-nanoparticle technology has shown robust immune responses and protection against viral challenges, similar to the concept used in COVID-19 vaccines, and this technology is being explored for ASF vaccines [17]. Furthermore, the development of mRNA vaccines targeting specific viral proteins identified as critical for inducing protective immune responses could provide a potent and promising vaccine candidate for controlling ASF [2]. We recommend that stakeholders in veterinary medicine, public health, and international cooperation prioritize the development, testing, and implementation of new vaccine technologies, including mRNA vaccines, to combat ASF. International cooperation is crucial given the global impact of ASF on swine industries and food security [5], and we highlight the need for further research into gene-deleted live-attenuated vaccines as well, which have demonstrated safety and efficacy in certain conditions [4].


